    "matching_sentences": [
      {
        "id": "en1b5ff9f-en1b51848",
        "raw": "AHĪRWĀṬĪ ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b5d0ec-en1b512f4",
        "raw": "Ahīrwāṭī , also called Hīrwāṭī and Ahīrwāl ( or the language of the Ahīr country ) is spoken in the west of the district of Gurgaon ( including the state of Pataudi ) ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b53f15-en1b55014",
        "raw": "Further north we have the Bāṅgarū dialect of Western Hindī , and the Ahīrwāṭī of Delhi and Rohtak , is much mixed with this dialect ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b506b5-en1b5e815",
        "raw": "To the east of Ahīrwāṭī , in the central part of Gurgaon , and also south of Gurgaon , in the state of Alwar , the language is Mēwātī , of which Ahīrwāṭī is merely a variety ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b5d569-en1b5777f",
        "raw": "We may take the centre of the Ahīrwāṭī-speaking country as Rewari in West Gurgaon ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b58c57-en1b5751b",
        "raw": "Here , except in the north of the tract , where Bāṅgarū is spoken , the language is also Ahīrwāṭī ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b5a995-en1b525b7",
        "raw": "In Shēkhāwāṭī it is a form of Mārwāṛī ; in Tōrāwāṭī it is a form of Jaipurī ; in Alwar it is Mēwātī ; and in south Nabha it is Ahīrwāṭī ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b5005e-en1b5e312",
        "raw": "The language of Narnaul of Patiala is also Ahīrwātī , but , as may be expected , it is much mixed with the surrounding dialects ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b597af-en1b5fe26",
        "raw": "We thus see that Ahīrwāṭī represents the connecting link between Mēwātī and three other dialects , Bāṅgarū , Bāgṛī , and Shēkhāwāṭī ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b55bf9-en1b5cd48",
        "raw": "The Ābhīras , or , as they are called at the present day , Ahīrs , or Hīrs , were once a most important tribe of Western India ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b53dc9-en1b54e23",
        "raw": "When the Kāttīs arrived in Gujarat in the 8th century they found the country in the hands of the Ahīrṣ The Ahīrs owned Khandesh and Nimar , and a shepherd chief of their tribe named Āsā is said to have founded the fort of Āsīrgarh in the latter district at the time of the Muhammadan in vasion ."
      },
      {
        "id": "en1b560c9-en1b52384",
        "raw": "Ptolemy mentioned them under the name of ’ Α βίρια and , at the beginning of our era , there were Ahīr rājās so far east as Nepal ."
      }
    ]
  },
